# What EAS uploads.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE EDITING. The moment this file exists, eas-cli STOPS reading
# every .gitignore in the repo — root and nested alike — and uses only these
# rules. So this file has to restate all of them, and any rule that is forgotten
# here silently ships something that should not be shipped, or (far worse) is
# silently DROPPED from the archive while the build still succeeds.
#
# That is not hypothetical. BSV Browser lost its entire Android Nearby module from
# every EAS archive for three days (2026-07-29 → 07-31) because its .easignore had
# gone stale against .gitignore. The build was green the whole time.
#
# The file exists because three things this build CANNOT work without are
# deliberately gitignored, being large generated artifacts:
#
#   apps/mobile/assets/ui/                       28 MB  — the chrome itself.
#     app.json sets chromeUrl:"bundled" with no network fallback, and the
#     "Bundle Nexus UI" run script exits 1 when it is absent.
#   packages/react-native-secp-native/ios/*.xcframework    120 MB
#   packages/react-native-engine-native/ios/*.xcframework  140 MB
#     SecpNative.podspec:11 and EngineNative.podspec:17 RAISE when these are
#     missing, so pod install dies rather than degrading.
#
# Everything below mirrors the .gitignore rules verbatim EXCEPT those three.
#
# To verify after any edit:
#   git ls-files --exclude-from .easignore --ignored --cached
# It should list ONLY the three paths above. Anything else in that output is a
# file you are about to strip from a build.

node_modules/
.expo/
dist/
build/
web-build/
*.log
.DS_Store
.env.local
apps/desktop/out/
# Desktop's copy of the chrome. The mobile copy above IS uploaded; this one is not
# used by any EAS build and would double the tarball.
apps/desktop/ui/
apps/mobile/.expo/

# Generated native projects. Path-anchored, exactly as in .gitignore — an
# unanchored `android/` here would also match
# packages/react-native-localpay-transport/android, which is REAL SOURCE and is
# the whole nearby rail on that platform. That is the three-day bug, precisely.
/apps/mobile/ios/
/apps/mobile/android/

# Local build artifacts — never upload (tens of MB each)
*.ipa
*.aab
*.apk

# apps/ui IS our source and is uploaded. Only its build products are not.
apps/ui/node_modules/
apps/ui/.next/
apps/ui/out/

# Rust/native build output. Note what is NOT here: ios/*.xcframework, which
# .gitignore excludes and this build requires.
packages/react-native-*/node_modules/
packages/react-native-*/android/build/
packages/react-native-*/android/.cxx/
native-*/target/
native-secp-poc/sdk-2.1.6/
native-secp-poc/packages/SecpNativeFFI/SecpNative.xcframework/
native-engine-ffi/packages/EngineNativeFFI/EngineNative.xcframework/

# NOT excluded: the native-*/ Rust sources themselves. Dropping them looks free —
# the xcframeworks are prebuilt and ship above, so nothing compiles Rust during an
# EAS build — but the podspecs' self-heal fallback (scripts/sync-xcframework.sh)
# reads out of native-secp-poc/ and native-engine-ffi/, and a tarball where that
# fallback cannot run is one where a missing framework becomes unrecoverable
# instead of merely slow. They are ~35 small tracked files; the 1.4 GB is all in
# target/, excluded above.
